Load-bearing trick: mid-call tool calls. Agent 2's log_quote_item / log_outcome fire during the call so quotes land itemized; Agent 3's get_best_bid returns the real lowest competing bid mid-call → leverage is DB data, not script → price movement is emergent (the #1 criterion). Keeping Caller and Closer separate keeps each prompt simple and maps 1:1 to the brief's scored modules.

Closer context note (Maxim): the Closer must know who it's calling and their round-1 quote — pass company_name and opening_total as EL dynamic variables when starting the session, so it never has to ask and never invents.


Config: verticals/cleaning.yaml
Spec fields: property (type, sqft, BR/BA, levels) · clean_type (standard/deep/move_out) · frequency · condition (clutter, pets, weeks since last clean) · add_ons (fridge, oven, windows, baseboards, laundry) · access (parking, entry, walk-up floor) · schedule.

Benchmarks (2026 US): standard 2–3BR $120–$280 (avg ~$180) · deep = 1.5–2x standard ($200–$500+) · move-out $250–$600 · hourly $45–$75/cleaner (companies) · recurring discounts: weekly ~20–25%, biweekly ~10–15%.

Red flags: >30% below median for clean type · standard-clean price on a first-time job (market prices first cleans as deep → on-arrival upcharge risk) · uncapped hourly · refuses to itemize · cash-only/no written quote.

Levers: cite competing binding quote · commit biweekly for discount · bundle add-ons · off-peak slot.


