Per-person step-by-step
Umra — Backend core (FastAPI + Supabase)
22:00 Write and post job_spec.json + quote.json schemas in the repo README (15 min — blocks everyone, do first). Create Supabase tables: specs, companies (seed 3 mock cleaning companies), calls, quotes, quote_items. (You do NOT build POST /specs — that's Aleks's. You own everything downstream of a spec existing.)
22:30 FastAPI skeleton + routes: PATCH /specs/{id}/confirm (the gate), GET /specs/{id}, GET /report/{spec_id}.
23:00 The three tool endpoints (EL calls these as webhooks — dead simple):
POST /tools/log_quote_item {call_id, label, amount}
GET /tools/get_best_bid?spec_id= → current lowest confirmed total + company name
POST /tools/log_outcome {call_id, outcome: quote|callback|declined, total, pricing_model}
23:30 Call-session state machine: POST /calls/start (spec_id, company_id) → queued→calling→done; GET /calls?spec_id= for Nirma's polling.
00:00 Red-flag + ranking engine as a pure Python function over cleaning.yaml; runs on log_outcome; writes flags to the quote row. Ranking = total asc, flagged quotes demoted with reason.
00:30 Checkpoint support: text loop end-to-end against your endpoints.
00:30–02:15 Pair with Maxim on EL webhook wiring (auth headers, payload shapes); capture negotiation fields (opening_total, final_total, moved_because) from tool calls.
02:15–03:00 Report payload: transcript URL + recording URL per quote, null-tolerant. Freeze.
Aleks — Intake backend + conversation design + EL dashboard + personas
22:00 Finalize the 3 persona cards with exact numbers (15 min). Skim cleaning.yaml.
22:15 — Intake backend (your endpoints, in Umra's FastAPI app):
POST /intake/doc — accept PDF/photo → Claude vision → draft job_spec.json, validated against Umra's schema, missing fields marked.
POST /specs — persist any spec (from doc route OR voice-interview transcript) to Supabase; single write-path for all intake.
Test with 2 fixtures: a phone photo of a room, a fake existing-quote PDF. Target: working by 23:45. Use Claude Code — this is ~2 focused hours of vibe-codeable work.
23:45 Write the four required behavior artifacts (short, paste-able):
Disclosure: "Hi, I'm an AI assistant calling on behalf of a customer to get a cleaning quote…"
"Are you a robot?": honest yes + straight back to the quote, warm not defensive.
Honesty guardrail: cite only bids returned by get_best_bid; never invent scope/bids.
Structured endings: itemized quote / callback commitment / documented decline — never "around two hundred."
00:00 System prompts — three, one per agent: (a) Agent 1 Interviewer — asks what a professional estimator asks, fills every spec field, confirms back; (b) Agent 2 Caller — describes the confirmed spec identically, collects the first quote, presses for fee itemization, no negotiating beyond clarifying, ends structured; (c) Agent 3 Closer — opens from the company's round-1 quote (dynamic variable), pulls get_best_bid, cites the real bid, applies levers, honesty guardrail hard-coded. Hand all three to Maxim for text-loop testing.
00:15 Configure all three agents in the ElevenLabs dashboard (with Maxim): paste each prompt, attach each agent's tools per the table above, pick voices (same voice for Caller and Closer — it's the same 'assistant' to the companies), set first messages.
00:30–02:15 Role-play window, two rounds: Round 1 — take Agent 2's calls as each persona back-to-back; give the first price when asked, create friction (interrupt, go vague, ask "am I talking to a robot?"), reveal fees only if pressed. Round 2 — take Agent 3's callback as the premium persona; only drop $420→~$355 when the Closer actually cites the competing bid — that's the money moment.
02:15–03:00 Best takes re-recorded as golden calls; note timestamps of the price-drop and disclosure moments. Freeze.
Maxim — Voice integration + negotiation loop (Claude Code throughout)
22:00 EL account + API key in .env; skim EL Agents docs: tools/webhooks + web-call widget. Aleks owns dashboard config; you own everything programmatic.
22:30 — Text negotiation loop (moved to you): run Aleks's Caller and Closer prompts (drafts until 00:00) against a Claude counter-agent playing each persona — Caller for round-1 extraction, Closer for the bid-citing renegotiation. 8-case eval: every fee extracted? lowball flag triggered? Closer cites only real DB bids? ending structured? Feed findings back to Aleks between his intake sprints.
23:30 Wire EL agent tools → Umra's three endpoints; verify a dashboard test call writes rows to Supabase. Riskiest integration in the build — front-load it.
00:15 Embed/link Agent 1 (Interviewer) for live on-recording interaction; pipe its transcript → spec extraction → Aleks's POST /specs (same write-path as docs → identical spec from both paths, a scored criterion).
00:30 Drive the checkpoint run.
00:30–02:15 Round 1 — Agent 2 (Caller) web-call sessions vs Aleks: start a call per company card, tie EL session → call_id, save recording + transcript URLs onto the quote at call end. Cap test calls ~90s. Then wire Round 2 — Agent 3 (Closer) session start with company_name + opening_total passed as dynamic variables.
02:15–03:00 Run the Closer renegotiation vs the premium persona: Agent 3 pulls get_best_bid, cites it, premium drops; negotiation fields (opening→final, moved_because) land on the quote. Confirm recordings play from the report. Freeze.
Morning (you own): run-through, record the 3-min demo, README, submit by 13:00.
Nirma — Frontend (React + Tailwind)
22:00 Vite + React + Tailwind scaffold. Copy Umra's schemas; create fixtures/ with 1 spec + 3 quotes so you never block.
22:30 — Screen 1 (Intake): dropzone (PDF/image → Aleks's POST /intake/doc) + "Start voice interview" button + parsing state.
23:00 — Screen 2 (Spec confirmation — the required gate): editable spec form; big unmistakable "Confirm & call 3 companies" CTA → Umra's PATCH /specs/{id}/confirm. Judges must see this gate.
23:45 — Screen 3 (Call board, per-call updates): one card per company, status pill (queued / calling / done). Poll GET /calls?spec_id= every ~5s; on completion the card flips to total, itemized fees, red-flag badge, outcome. "Best bid so far" ticker updates per completion.
00:30 Swap fixtures for live API; checkpoint.
00:45 — Screen 4 (Ranked report): ranked table (total, expandable fees, flag badges) · recommended-deal card with plain-language "why" · opening → final price-drop callout · transcript link + audio player per quote.
02:00–03:00 Projector polish: big type, high contrast, price numbers are the hero. Freeze.


Demo script (~3 min, recorded 10:30–12:00 with live interaction)
20s pain: "Same 2BR deep clean. Three companies. Watch the prices."
Intake, live (Agent 1): interact with the Interviewer unscripted ~20s and drop the photo — both produce the identical spec → hit Confirm.
Round 1 (Agent 2): three Caller calls vs Aleks's personas; each completed card flips with itemized fees; lowballer gets the red flag.
Round 2, the money moment (Agent 3), played aloud: the Closer calls the premium company back, cites the competing bid, premium drops $420→$355. Replay the "are you a robot?" clip.
Report: ranked table, lowballer flagged −38% + hidden fees, recommended deal with transcript citation + the opening→final price-drop callout.
Scope traps — refuse
Twilio/real businesses · agent-to-agent audio · real Google Places (mock the list) · a second working vertical (show the yaml for 10s) · auth/deploy/mobile · streaming transcripts (cut by design).
If behind at 01:30 (pre-agreed cuts, in order)
Voice interview → shortest viable live interaction (2 questions) with doc intake carrying the full spec.
Drop friction extras; keep disclosure + robot-question + the Agent 3 price-drop call (never cut this — it's the #1 criterion).
Screen 4 citations → static links, no in-app player.
Nuclear: submit the 00:30 text loop + whatever golden calls exist.

